    "    <div style=\"font-size:1.85em; font-weight:750; line-height:1.2; margin:8px 0;\">Riverbed evolution: model background and first experiment</div>\n",
    "    <div><strong>Shawn Chartrand</strong><br/>School of Environmental Science · Simon Fraser University<br/>Landscape Dynamics Lab</div>\n",
    "  </div>\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main question:** How does a change in upstream sediment supply affect the bed elevation of a gravel-bed river?\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook introduces the equations used in a simple one-dimensional morphodynamic model. We will first connect water flow to sediment transport, and then connect sediment transport to changes in the riverbed. The calculations use a **constant channel width**, a **single representative grain size**, and **steady, normal-flow hydraulics** at each time step.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Learning goals:** (1) explain the normal-depth approximation; (2) calculate the role of Shields stress in bedload transport; (3) interpret the Exner equation and its numerical form; and (4) predict whether a riverbed will aggrade or degrade when sediment supply changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook describes a simplified model of riverbed evolution. The model combines normal-flow hydraulics, a single representative grain size, a Meyer-Peter–Müller-type bedload relation, and sediment continuity. It does not represent backwater effects, mixed-grain transport, or bed-surface sorting."
   ],
   "id": "05fd88fe"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Water flow: the normal-depth approximation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Physical idea.** Flow depth and velocity determine the stress acting on the bed. We use normal flow so students can focus on sediment transport and bed change.\n",
    "\n",
    "For a wide, rectangular channel with constant width $B$, discharge $Q$ is related to mean velocity $U$ and flow depth $h$ by water continuity:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$Q = BUh, \\qquad q_w = \\frac{Q}{B}=Uh.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here $q_w$ is water discharge per unit channel width (m$^2$/s). A more general description of steady, gradually varied flow is given by the depth-gradient equation (Parker, 2007):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\frac{dh}{dx}=\\frac{S-S_f}{1-Fr^2},$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $S=-d\\eta/dx$ is the local bed slope, $S_f$ is the friction slope, and $Fr=U/\\sqrt{gh}$ is the Froude number. **For normal (uniform) flow**, $dh/dx=0$, so $S_f=S$. This avoids solving the backwater equation and is the central hydraulic simplification in our teaching model.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use a Manning–Strickler resistance relation (Parker, 2007):\n",
    "\n",
    "$$C_f=\\frac{1}{\\alpha_r^2}\\left(\\frac{h}{k_s}\\right)^{-1/3}, \\qquad S_f=\\frac{C_fU^2}{gh},$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $k_s$ is roughness height, $C_f$ is a dimensionless resistance coefficient, and $\\alpha_r=8.1$. Substitute $U=q_w/h$ and set $S_f=S$. Rearranging gives **normal depth**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boxed{h_n=\\left(\\frac{k_s^{1/3}q_w^2}{\\alpha_r^2gS}\\right)^{3/10}}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The model evaluates this expression using the local, positive bed slope. It then calculates $U=q_w/h_n$. The model does not calculate backwater effects, hydraulic jumps, or flow over adverse (upstream-rising) bed slopes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Think about it:** For fixed $Q$, $B$, and roughness, should flow depth increase or decrease as the channel becomes steeper?"
   ],
   "id": "31397d17"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. From flow to bedload transport\n",
    "\n",
    "**Physical idea.** The Shields parameter compares fluid forcing with the submerged weight of sediment grains. Transport increases once the critical threshold is exceeded.\n",
    "\n",
    "Under the wide-channel normal-flow approximation, bed shear stress and shear velocity are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\tau_b=\\rho_w gh_nS, \\qquad u_* = \\sqrt{gh_nS}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We express the stress relative to the submerged weight of a representative sediment grain using the **Shields parameter**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boxed{\\theta=\\frac{\\tau_b}{(\\rho_s-\\rho_w)gD}=\\frac{h_nS}{RD}}, \\qquad R=\\frac{\\rho_s}{\\rho_w}-1.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here $D$ is the representative grain diameter, $\\rho_s$ and $\\rho_w$ are sediment and water densities, and $R=1.65$ in the model. Larger $\\theta$ generally indicates greater potential for grain motion.\n",
    "\n",
    "The teaching model uses a **Meyer-Peter–Müller-type excess-Shields relation** (Meyer-Peter and Müller, 1948) for volumetric bedload transport per unit width:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boxed{q_b=3.97\\,\\sqrt{RgD}\\,D\\,\\max(\\theta-\\theta_c,0)^{3/2}}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Here $q_b$ has units of m$^2$/s and $\\theta_c$ is the critical Shields stress (default $0.045$). The factor $3.97$ is the coefficient implemented in **this teaching model**; published Meyer-Peter–Müller formulations and calibrations may use different coefficients. If $\\theta\\leq\\theta_c$, this equation predicts zero bedload transport.\n",
    "\n",
    "This single-size formulation does not track individual grain-size fractions, hiding effects, or changes in surface grain-size distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Think about it:** Why might increasing discharge increase bedload transport much more rapidly than it increases mean flow velocity?"
   ],
   "id": "8f78c0f6"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Sediment continuity: the Exner equation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Physical idea.** Riverbed elevation changes when sediment transport into a reach differs from sediment transport out of it.\n",
    "\n",
    "Bedload entering and leaving a short river segment determines whether sediment accumulates or is removed. The one-dimensional Exner equation is (Exner, 1925; Parker, 2007)\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boxed{(1-\\lambda_p)\\frac{\\partial\\eta}{\\partial t}=-\\frac{\\partial q_b}{\\partial x}},$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $\\eta$ is bed elevation, $t$ is time, and $\\lambda_p$ is bed porosity. The factor $(1-\\lambda_p)$ is the **solid fraction** of the bed material.\n",
    "\n",
    "- If bedload transport **decreases downstream**, $\\partial q_b/\\partial x<0$: more sediment enters than leaves, so the bed **aggrades** ($\\partial\\eta/\\partial t>0$).\n",
    "- If bedload transport **increases downstream**, $\\partial q_b/\\partial x>0$: more sediment leaves than enters, so the bed **degrades** ($\\partial\\eta/\\partial t<0$).\n",
    "- If transport is the same everywhere, there is no bed elevation change from sediment-flux divergence.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a **mass balance**, not a rule that high transport rates necessarily produce erosion. A large but spatially constant $q_b$ produces no change in bed elevation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Think about it:** A reach transports a large amount of sediment, but the incoming and outgoing rates are equal. Does its bed elevation change?"
   ],
   "id": "9d516c37"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. How the computer solves Exner\n",
    "\n",
    "**Numerical idea.** The model conserves sediment by tracking fluxes through the upstream and downstream faces of each computational cell.\n",
    "\n",
    "Using a conservative finite-volume approach (LeVeque, 2002), the river is divided into cells of length $\\Delta x$. The model stores bed elevation $\\eta_i$ at the **centre** of each cell and computes bedload flux $q_{b,i+1/2}$ at its **faces**. The sediment balance over cell $i$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$ (1-\\lambda_p)\\,\\Delta x\\,\\frac{d\\eta_i}{dt}=q_{b,i-1/2}-q_{b,i+1/2}. $$\n",
    "\n",
    "Using a forward step in time, the **discretized Exner equation** is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\boxed{\\eta_i^{n+1}=\\eta_i^n-\\frac{\\Delta t}{(1-\\lambda_p)\\Delta x}\\left(q_{b,i+1/2}^n-q_{b,i-1/2}^n\\right)}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The superscript $n$ denotes the time step. Interior-face slopes are calculated from adjacent cell-centre elevations:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$S_{i+1/2}=\\frac{\\eta_i-\\eta_{i+1}}{\\Delta x}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "At each time step, the code uses this sequence:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Estimate local slopes at cell faces and compute normal depth and Shields stress.\n",
    "2. Calculate bedload transport capacity at each face.\n",
    "3. Prescribe the **upstream sediment feed**; use the calculated transport capacity at other faces.\n",
    "4. Update each cell's bed elevation using the Exner balance above.\n",
    "\n",
    "At the downstream end, the model extends the final interior slope to the outlet face. This is a simplified downstream boundary treatment. For a channel of constant width, the change in bed-sediment storage should equal upstream sediment input minus downstream sediment output (accounting for porosity).\n",
    "\n",
    "The model uses an adaptive time step and checks that slopes remain positive. Its morphodynamic stability estimate is\n",
    "\n",
    "$$C_m=\\frac{D_m\\Delta t}{\\Delta x^2},\\qquad D_m=\\frac{1}{1-\\lambda_p}\\frac{\\partial q_b}{\\partial S}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The code limits $C_m$ to a conservative value of $0.20$ when selecting the time step. This is a numerical safeguard, **not** proof that the model is accurate or converged. The hydraulic Courant number $C_h=U\\Delta t/\\Delta x$ is reported for reference; it does not control the quasi-steady hydraulic calculation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Think about it:** In the discrete equation, what happens to $\\eta_i$ when the upstream-face sediment flux is larger than the downstream-face flux?"
   ],
   "id": "56778d7c"
  },
